  "limitations": [
    "Suggestions apply best to the range of architectural levels employed and should be extrapolated to other levels with care.",
    "Individual differences were substantial, and the relative contribution of rating scale use versus actual differences in individual preference was not established.",
    "Subjects were Dartmouth undergraduates, whose preferences may not match those of other populations."
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This empirical study investigated room preference as a function of architectural features (ceiling height, ceiling slope, wall angle) and presumed user activities (e.g., reading, dancing, listening). Findings consistently showed single-peaked preference functions for ceiling height and wall angle, with a quadratic model effectively describing the relationship between preference and the logarithm of ceiling height, peaking around 10 feet. Ceiling slope exhibited a double-peaked preference. User activities significantly interacted with ceiling height to modulate preferences, with higher ceilings preferred for activities like listening. The general characteristics of preference functions were consistent across model rooms and a real-scale adjustable ceiling. Limitations include the generalizability of findings beyond the tested architectural ranges and student population, as well as the substantial individual differences observed."
}